    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2736.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 715,
      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is the most abundant and essential molecule for life on Earth as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses—chemical, physical, biological, and environmental.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical Definition\nChemically, water is a compound made of two types of atoms: **Hydrogen (H)** and **Oxygen (O)**. \n* Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. \n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom**.\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\nWater is unique because of how it behaves physically. It is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states:\n* **Liquid:** The water we drink, swim in, and rain from the sky.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water, which forms glaciers, snow, and ice cubes.\n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, fog, and steam.\n\n**Other remarkable physical properties include:**\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is why it is so good at transporting nutrients in living things.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing insects to walk on water) and to other things (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant trees).\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and get denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats on liquid water instead of sinking to the bottom of the ocean. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze solid from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n\n### 3. Biological Importance (Life)\nWater is the foundation of biology. \n* **Our Bodies:** The human body is made of about 60% water. The brain is about 73% water, and the lungs are about 83%. \n* **Cellular Processes:** All the chemical reactions that keep living things alive—metabolism, digestion, circulation—take place in water-based fluids inside cells.\n* **Evolution:** Scientists believe life itself originated in Earth's ancient oceans roughly 3.8 billion years ago.\n\n### 4. Environmental Importance (The Planet)\n* **The Earth's Surface:** About **71% of Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **Climate Regulation:** Water absorbs and releases heat slowly. Oceans act as the planet's thermostat, absorbing heat from the sun near the equator and circulating it toward the poles, which moderates global weather patterns.\n* **The Water Cycle (Hydrologic Cycle):** Water is constantly recycled through evaporation (turning into vapor), condensation (forming clouds), precipitation (rain and snow), and collection (flowing into rivers and oceans). The water you drink today is the same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple combination of two gases that, together, become a life-giving, shape-shifting liquid that covers most of our planet and makes existence possible."
